NEUTRALITY COVENANT

Confidior grades claims. It does not sell the ground being graded.

Prohibited for this project and the brand it carries:
  - selling silicon or chip design
  - selling cloud compute or inference capacity
  - holding custody of third parties' keys or secrets

Permitted and intended: grading, monitoring, independent assessment,
enforcement of grading outcomes (the Policy Gateway), the registry, and the
transparency log.

The reason, in one sentence: a party that sells the ground it grades has
something to gain from the answer, and a grade is only worth reading from a
party that does not.


BRAND RULE

The Confidior name belongs to the verifier only. Any other project by the
same founder carries a different name. Where a relationship exists, it is
disclosed plainly rather than hidden. The endorsement direction runs one
way: the verifier may grade another project publicly, with that project's
site carrying the badge; no other project's name appears here as an
ownership claim.


WHY THIS IS CHECKABLE

Independence here is mechanical, not contractual. The engine is open source,
its inputs (evidence, the attack database, reference values) are public, and
every verdict is re-runnable by anyone with the same evidence. A legal
structure adds procurement comfort later; it is not what makes a grade
trustworthy.


WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM

The covenant constrains what this project sells, not what it grades. It is no
statement about the correctness of any grade; for that, see the boundary
statement shipped with every verdict, and the engine's own documentation on
what it verifies and what it does not.
